red symbols.\n\nAlso: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we need to process nodes in descending depth order, and for equal depth, use node ID as tie break? Or \"use node ID as stable tie break at equal depth.\" So within same depth, sort by node ID (string). Then process each node: if removable, and if all required symbols still present in current retained set after removing this node and its descendants (since they are also removed if the node is removed), then remove it. Also we never remove the root, or nodes that are not removable, or nodes with retained children? Actually \"never remove ... a node with retained children.\" That means if a node has children that are retained (i.e., not removed), then we cannot remove that node. In the process, we remove nodes, so children might be removed before the parent. So when considering a parent, we need to check if any of its children are still retained. Since we process deeper first, children are considered before parent, so if a child is removable and removed, then it is not retained. So when we consider the parent, its children that were removed are not retained. So we need to check if the parent has any child that is not removed and is still in the tree. But the children might be non-removable (permanent). So we must keep those. So the condition: can only remove node if all its children are also being removed (i.e., they are not retained). But the spec says \"a node with retained children\" - if any child is retained, we cannot remove the node. So we cannot remove a node that has children that are not removed. Since we process deeper first, by the time we consider a node, we have decided on its children. So we can check: is there any child node that is still in the retained set? If yes, skip removal.\n\nAlso \"removable\" attribute says if the node can be considered for removal (but even if removable, we still need the symbol condition and children condition). The root is not removable. Required symbols: we must ensure that after removal, each required_symbol is present in at least one remaining node. The required_symbols are symbols of nodes. So we need to maintain a set of symbols present in the current retained nodes.\n\nWe also have utility values, but the description doesn't mention optimizing utility. It seems we just remove if possible, no utility trade-off. So it's a greedy removal in post-order with constraints.\n\nThus algorithm:\n- Build a dictionary mapping node id to node object.\n- Build a mapping from parent id to list of child ids (children).\n- Identify root: parent null.\n- Initialize set of retained node ids = all node ids initially? But we will remove nodes. Actually the process: we start with all nodes, then we consider nodes in order and possibly remove them. So initially retained = set of all node ids.\n- Initialize symbol set = set of symbols of all nodes.\n- Determine order: list of node ids sorted by (-depth, id). So deeper first, then id.\n- Loop over nodes in that order:\n  - if node is root (id == \"root\") skip.\n  - if not removable: skip.\n  - Check if any child is still retained: for each child id in children[node.id], if child id in retained, then cannot remove, skip.\n  - Check if removing this node and its descendants would still satisfy required_symbols: we need to compute the symbols that would be lost if we remove this node. The node itself has a symbol, and its descendants (if any) are removed along with it. But since we process deeper first, all descendants that are removable and will be removed have already been removed? Actually not: there may be descendants that are not removable, or that we chose not to remove (e.g., because they are required symbols). Those descendants would be retained and would prevent removal of the parent due to \"retained children\" condition. So if we get to the parent and we are considering removal, that means all its children are either already removed or are non-removable? Wait,