set or counts. Simpler: maintain a dictionary sym_to_count (number of retained nodes with that symbol). Initially count each symbol. When we remove a node, decrement count for its symbol. If count becomes 0 and that symbol is required, then removal is not allowed. So during candidate check: if node's symbol is in required_symbols and sym_count[node.symbol] == 1 (only this node has it), then cannot remove. Otherwise can.\n\nBut also nodes with multiple symbols? No, each node has exactly one symbol.\n\nSo algorithm steps:\n1. Parse case: nodes list, required_symbols list.\n2. Build node_by_id dict.\n3. Build children dict: for each node, if parent not null, add child id to parent's list.\n4. Determine root id: the node with parent null.\n5. Initialize retained = set of all node ids.\n6. Initialize sym_counts = defaultdict(int): for each node, sym_counts[node.symbol] += 1.\n7. Create list of node ids to process: all node ids except root (since root cannot be removed). For each node, compute depth from node.depth field. Sort by (-depth, id). \n8. For each node_id in that order:\n   - node = node_by_id[node_id]\n   - if node['removable'] is False: continue.\n   - Check children: if any child_id in retained: continue (cannot remove because would orphan retained child? Actually the spec says \"never remove ... a node with retained children\". So if any child is still in retained set, skip.\n   - Check symbol condition: symbol = node['symbol']; if symbol in required_symbols and sym_counts[symbol] == 1: skip (removing would lose required symbol).\n   - If passes both: remove node: retained.remove(node_id); sym_counts[symbol] -= 1; record node_id in removal_order list.\n   - Note: we don't need to recursively remove descendants because they are processed later? But we process deeper first, so descendants are processed before parent. So by the time we consider parent, descendants that are removable and pass conditions would have been removed already. If a descendant is not removable, it would still be in retained and block parent removal. So it's fine.\n9. After processing all nodes, removed_node_ids = removal_order (list of ids in order of removal).\n10. retained_node_ids = sorted(retained) (sorted by id string).\n11. Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_node_ids, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids}.\n\nCheck with given examples? We don't have expected output. But we can reason about first fixture:\nNodes:\n- root: depth0, removable=False, symbol root\n- n1: depth1, removable=False, parent root, symbol s1\n- n2: depth1, removable=True, parent root, symbol s2\n- n3: depth1, removable=False, parent root, symbol s0\n- n4: depth2, removable=True, parent n2, symbol s1\n- n5: depth2, removable=False, parent n3, symbol s2\n- n6: depth2, removable=True, parent n1, symbol s0\nRequired: root, s1\n\nProcessing order: depth descending: depth2 nodes: n4, n5, n6. Then depth1: n1, n2, n3. Depth0 root not processed.\nn4: removable True, parent n2. children: none? (n4 has no children). Check retained children: none. Symbol s1. sym_counts: s1 appears on n1 and n4 (2). required includes s1; sym_counts[s1]==2 >1, so can remove. Remove n4. retained loses n4. sym_counts[s1] becomes 1. removal_order=[n4].\nn5: removable False -> skip.\nn6: removable True, parent n1, children none. Symbol s0. required symbols: root, s1 (s0 not required). sym_counts[s0] initially 2 (n3 and n6). After removing n6, sym_counts[s0] becomes 1. That's fine. Check retained children: none. So remove n6. removal_order=[n4, n6].\nNow depth1 nodes: n1 (removable False skip), n2 (removable True, parent root, children: n4? but n4 removed, so no retained children. Symbol s2, not required. sym_counts[s2] currently: only n2? n2 has s2, n5 has s2? n5 is retained (non-removable). So sym_counts[s2]=2 initially. After removal, becomes 1, still not required, fine. So remove n2. removal_order=[n4,n6,n2].\nn3: removable False skip.\nRoot not processed.\nFinal retained: root, n1, n3, n5. Sorted ids: n1, n3, n5, root? but root is \"root\". Sort strings: \"n1\", \"n3\", \"n5\", \"root\". Actual order: 'n1', 'n3', 'n5', 'root'. Removed: n4, n6, n2.\nCheck required symbols: root present, s1 present on n1. Good. The node n2 removed, its descendants? n4 already removed. So tree structure? Not needed.\n\nNow check if there is any condition about \"parent must have all children removed before parent can be removed\"? We already check retained children. In this case n2 had child n4 which is removed, so fine.\n\nBut we also need to consider that n1 is not removable, so n6 (child) was removed, but that's fine.\n\nSo algorithm works.\n\nNow implement solve(case). Need to use only standard library. Do not mutate case. Return dict with keys removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nEdge cases: nodes may not have depth field? It's given. Use node['depth'].\n\nThe order: deeper first, then by id. Use sorted with key=lambda nid: (-node_by_id[nid]['depth'], nid). But careful: depth can be integer, negative sign for descending.\n\nRetained children check: for each child of node (from children dict), if child id in retained set, skip.\n\nPotential issue: The children dict should be built from parent field. But we need to ensure that if a node's parent is not in the nodes list? Assume valid.\n\nWe must also consider that some nodes might have no children, fine.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Use from collections import defaultdict for sym_counts.\n- Build node_by_id = {node['id']: node for node in nodes}\n- Build children = defaultdict(list); for node in nodes: if node['parent']: children[node['parent']].append(node['id'])\n- root = next(node for node in nodes if node['parent'] is None) or find by id 'root'? Use parent is None.\n\n- retained = set(node['id'] for node in nodes)\n- sym_counts = defaultdict(int); for node in nodes: sym_counts[node['symbol']] += 1\n- required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols']) for O(1) membership.\n- process_nodes = [node['id'] for node in nodes if node['id'] != root['id']] (or root id). Actually we have root id from root.\n- sort: process_nodes.sort(key=lambda nid: (-node_by_id[nid]['depth'], nid))\n- removal_order = []\n- for nid in process_nodes:\n    node = node_by_id[nid]\n    if not node['removable']: continue\n    # check retained children\n    if any(child in retained for child in children.get(nid, [])): continue\n    # check symbol condition\n    sym = node['symbol']\n    if sym in required_symbols and sym_counts[sym] == 1: continue\n    # remove\n    retained.remove(nid)\n    sym_counts[sym] -= 1\n    removal_order.append(nid)\n- retained_node_ids = sorted(retained)\n- return {\"removed_node_ids\": removal_order, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids}\n\nTest with first fixture: expected (we computed) removed: ['n4','n6','n2']? But our order: n4, n6, n2 matches. retained: ['n1','n3','n5','root'] sorted: ['n1','n3','n5','root']. Valid.\n\nNow for second and third fixtures, similar but numbers different. Should work.\n\nNow we need to produce the implementation as a Python source string, embedded in a JSON object with key \"implementation\". The entire response must be that JSON.\n\nSo we need to write the Python code as a string, escaping newlines etc.\n\nLet's write the solve function.\n\nOne nuance: The case payload may have other keys, but we only need nodes and required_symbols. Use case['nodes'], case['required_symbols'].\n\nAlso note: The spec says \"Do not mutate the input.\" So we use copies.\n\nNow write the code. Use triple-quoted string? In JSON string, we need to escape backslashes and quotes. We'll write the function and then stringify.\n\nImplementation:\n\n```python\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    # Build mappings\n    node_by_id = {node['id']: node for node in nodes}\n    children = defaultdict(list)\n    for node in nodes:\n        parent = node['parent']\n        if parent is not None:\n            children[parent].append(node['id'])\n    # Find root\n    root_id = None\n    for node in nodes:\n        if node['parent'] is None:\n            root_id = node['id']\n            break\n    # Initialize retained and symbol counts\n    retained = set(node['id'] for node in nodes)\n    sym_counts = defaultdict(int)\n    for node in nodes:\n        sym_counts[node['symbol']] += 1\n    # Order: all non-root nodes, by depth descending, then id\n    process_ids = [node['id'] for node in nodes if node['id'] != root_id]\n    process_ids.sort(key=lambda nid: (-node_by_id[nid]['depth'], nid))\n    removal_order = []\n    for nid in process_ids:\n        node = node_by_id[nid]\n        if not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        # Check retained children\n        if any(child in retained for child in children.get(nid, [])):\n            continue\n        # Check symbol condition\n        sym = node['symbol']\n        if sym in required_symbols and sym_counts[sym] == 1:\n            continue\n        # Remove node\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        sym_counts[sym] -= 1\n        removal_order.append(nid)\n    retained_node_ids = sorted(retained)\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removal_order, 'retained_node_ids': retained_node_ids}\n```\n\nNow embed as JSON string. Write the entire response as {\"implementation\": \"...\"}. Escape newlines as \\n, backslashes as \\\\, quotes as \\\", tabs etc. We can also use raw string in Python to generate but easier to manually escape? Since the output must be a JSON, we can use Python's json.dumps to produce the envelope.\n\nBut the instruction: \"Your entire response must be exactly one valid JSON object with the single key 'implementation'. ... Encode the Python source as a JSON string, escaping newlines, backslashes, and quotes as required by JSON.\"\n\nSo we can build the Python source as a string, then use json.dumps to produce the final JSON. But we are to output the JSON directly as response. So in our answer, we should produce the JSON text. Since we are a language model, we can generate the JSON string with proper escaping. To avoid mistakes, we can write the Python code as a multi-line string and then use json.dumps in our mind? Alternatively, we can provide the JSON manually.\n\nI'll write the JSON:\n\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"from collections import defaultdict\\n\\ndef solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_by_id = {node['id']: node for node in nodes}\\n    children = defaultdict(list)\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        parent = node['parent']\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children[parent].append(node['id'])\\n    root_id = None\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        if node['parent'] is None:\\n            root_id = node['id']\\n            break\\n    retained = set(node['id'] for node in nodes)\\n    sym_counts = defaultdict(int)\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        sym_counts[node['symbol']] += 1\\n    process_ids = [node['id'] for node in nodes if node['id'] != root_id]\\n    process_ids.sort(key=lambda nid: (-node_by_id[nid]['depth'], nid))\\n    removal_order = []\\n    for nid in process_ids:\\n        node = node_by_id[nid]\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if any(child in retained for child in children.get(nid, [])):\\n            continue\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        if sym in required_symbols and sym_counts[sym] == 1:\\n            continue\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        sym_counts[sym] -= 1\\n        removal_order.append(nid)\\n    retained_node_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removal_order, 'retained_node_ids': retained_node_ids}\"\n}\n\nBut need to ensure that all quotes inside the Python source are properly escaped: In JSON, double quotes inside the string must be preceded by backslash. So we have many double quotes in the Python code (e.g., node['id'] becomes node['id']). In JSON, we need to escape the single quotes? No, single quotes are fine in JSON string, they don't need escaping. But double quotes inside the string must be escaped as \\\". However, our Python code uses both single quotes for dictionary keys and double quotes for strings like 'removable'? Actually we use single quotes for keys in the Python code: node['id'], case['nodes'], etc. Those are single quotes, not double. So they don't need escaping in JSON. However, we have double quotes in the return dictionary: return {'removed_node_ids': removal_order, 'retained_node_ids': retained_node_ids} uses single quotes. So no double quotes. But there is also the string 'root'? No. And f-strings? No. So the Python code does not contain double quotes, so no escaping needed except for backslashes? The code has backslash in line continuation? None. So the only escaping needed is newlines and backslashes. Backslashes: none. So we can write the JSON string with actual newlines escaped as \\n. But we need to ensure the JSON is a single line? Not necessarily, but typically we produce a compact JSON. The instruction says \"encoding newlines\". We can put actual newlines in the JSON string, but then the JSON parser would treat them as control characters? Actually JSON strings cannot contain literal newlines; they must be escaped as \\n. So we must replace each newline in the Python source with \\n. So our source string should be a single line or with \\n escape sequences.\n\nThus we need to write the Python code with \\n for line breaks. Let's do that.\n\nWe'll produce the JSON as a single line. But it's easier to construct in mind.\n\nI'll write the Python source as a string with \\n escapes. Then wrap in JSON.\n\nLet me generate:\n\n\"from collections import defaultdict\\n\\ndef solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_by_id = {node['id']: node for node in nodes}\\n    children = defaultdict(list)\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        parent = node['parent']\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children[parent].append(node['id'])\\n    root_id = None\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        if node['parent'] is None:\\n            root_id = node['id']\\n            break\\n    retained = set(node['id'] for node in nodes)\\n    sym_counts = defaultdict(int)\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        sym_counts[node['symbol']] += 1\\n    process_ids = [node['id'] for node in nodes if node['id'] != root_id]\\n    process_ids.sort(key=lambda nid: (-node_by_id[nid]['depth'], nid))\\n    removal_order = []\\n    for nid in process_ids:\\n        node = node_by_id[nid]\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if any(child in retained for child in children.get(nid, [])):\\n            continue\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        if sym in required_symbols and sym_counts[sym] == 1:\\n            continue\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        sym_counts[sym] -= 1\\n        removal_order.append(nid)\\n    retained_node_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removal_order, 'retained_node_ids': retained_node_ids}\"\n\nNow we put this into JSON: {\"implementation\": \"....\"} with the above string.\n\nWe must be careful with backslashes: the only backslash is in \\n and maybe if there is any. Also note that the Python code uses backslash for line continuation? No.\n\nAlso note that the JSON string must escape any double quotes, but there are none. So it's fine.\n\nNow produce final answer as a JSON object with that implementation string.\n\nI'll output the JSON."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2142,"completion_tokens":5958,"total_tokens":8100,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":5584},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":94},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}